1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,512
Why Is the Sky Blue?

2
00:00:02,462 --> 00:00:11,425
Sunlight consists of electromagnetic radiation across the visible spectrum, appearing white when all wavelengths are present in roughly equal intensity.

3
00:00:11,425 --> 00:00:28,312
As sunlight enters Earth's atmosphere, it interacts with gas molecules — primarily nitrogen (78%) and oxygen (21%) — through Rayleigh scattering, named after British physicist Lord Rayleigh, who published the mathematical formulation in 1871.

4
00:00:28,312 --> 00:00:33,587
The scattering cross-section is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the wavelength (λ⁻⁴).

5
00:00:33,587 --> 00:00:42,650
Blue light at approximately 450 nanometers is scattered roughly ten times more efficiently than red light at 650 nanometers.

6
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:48,875
An observer looking at the sky away from the direct solar beam sees predominantly the scattered blue component.

7
00:00:48,875 --> 00:00:59,650
The phenomenon is distinct from Mie scattering, which involves particles comparable in size to the wavelength (such as water droplets and dust), and produces the white appearance of clouds.

8
00:00:59,650 --> 00:01:08,187
At sunrise and sunset, sunlight traverses a much longer path through the atmosphere — roughly 30 times the atmospheric thickness at noon.

9
00:01:08,187 --> 00:01:21,375
The increased optical depth results in nearly complete removal of blue wavelengths through multiple scattering events, leaving the longer red and orange wavelengths (600-700 nm) to reach the observer's line of sight.

10
00:01:21,375 --> 00:01:31,862
This mechanism also explains why the sky on Mars, with its thin carbon dioxide atmosphere and suspended iron oxide dust, appears butterscotch-colored during daytime.

11
00:01:31,862 --> 00:01:38,887
On the Moon, which lacks any significant atmosphere, the sky remains perpetually black regardless of solar angle.

